# Findings that have been dealt with. One line retires exactly one finding of
# secrets:history, the scheduled full-history scan.
#
# This is not a baseline. --baseline-path silences every current finding at once, including
# credentials that are still live on their provider, which is the opposite of what that job
# is for. A line here is the other thing: a record that one specific value is now worthless.
#
# Uncomment a line only once the credential behind it has been ROTATED AT ITS PROVIDER, and
# put the date on it. Editing a secret out of a file does not unpublish it -- every value
# below is still in the history and in every clone that has ever been made, so nothing except
# the provider can retire it. This file is what gives the job an end: it is red today, one
# line less red per rotation, and green when the last one is done.
#
# The rotation list lives in docs/TODO.md and is longer than this file. Three of the
# credentials in commit 245174c are invisible to gitleaks' rule set -- the reasons are written
# down next to the list -- so five quiet fingerprints here would mean the scanner has nothing
# left to say, not that the list is finished.
#
# Every fingerprint below names the rotation row it retires -- R1..R7 in the table under
# "My next steps" in docs/TODO.md. Twelve fingerprints for seven credentials, because a
# fingerprint is per RULE and per COMMIT: one value can need several lines, and silencing one
# rule leaves the others. Uncomment every line of a row together, or the job stays red for a
# credential that has in fact been rotated.

